## Summary

Built and meshed `/work/20260911-114153-7b20/bash-t7`:

- **Domain**: 100×60×40 mm sealed box (x×y×z, z vertical), meshed with `blockMesh` at a uniform 1 mm cell size → 100×60×40 = 240,000 hex cells. Uniform 1 mm cells were chosen deliberately so the 20 mm cube zones land on exact cell boundaries (20 cells/side), with no partial/cut cells.
- **Boundaries**: all six faces are `wall` type patches — `floor` (z=0), `ceiling` (z=0.04), `xmin`, `xmax`, `ymin`, `ymax` — no inlet/outlet, matching the sealed-cavity spec. (No BC values assigned yet — that's for the next step when you attach the solver.)
- **Cell zones**: created via `topoSet`/`boxToCell` + `setToCellZone`:
  - `heater`: 20 mm cube sitting on the floor, x∈[0.01,0.03], y∈[0.02,0.04] (centered), z∈[0,0.02] — near the x=0 end.
  - `cooler`: 20 mm cube hanging from the ceiling, x∈[0.07,0.09], y∈[0.02,0.04] (centered), z∈[0.02,0.04] — near the x=0.1 end.
  - Both verified by `checkMesh`: exactly 8000 cells each (20×20×20), volume 8×10⁻⁶ m³, correct bounding boxes.
- **Mesh quality**: `checkMesh -allGeometry -allTopology` reports "Mesh OK" — zero non-orthogonality, zero skewness, aspect ratio 1 (it's a uniform Cartesian grid), no concave cells.
- Renders `mesh_overview.png` and `mesh_side.png` in the case dir confirm placement visually — heater on the floor at one end, cooler on the ceiling at the opposite end, diagonally placed as intended.

